// =============================================================================
// MemQL Automation Reference
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for `automation` syntax. It is NOT loaded
// by the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to
// skip it -- see core/dslfs/walker.go). Two tests parse it:
// component/memql/reference_skeletons_test.go and
// component/memql/sense/reference_diagnose_test.go.
//
// `automation` is the behavioral DSL's ONLY reactive + composing construct.
// From the behavioral-constructs ADR
// (docs/internal/design/dsl-behavioral-constructs-adr.md, §2):
//
//     logic decides, mutations persist, actions touch the world,
//     queries read, automations orchestrate and react.
//
// An automation is triggered by an event or a schedule (or invoked by the work
// run that names it, as a `@template`) and runs its statements in the order
// they are written. It is the one place the decide -> persist -> act rhythm
// comes together: call a `logic` to decide, a `mutation` to persist one
// write, an `action` to reach the outside world, a `query` to read, a
// `builtin` for a Go-backed operation, and a sub-`automation` to compose. It
// is also the only construct that may carry a `@trigger`, `publish` an event,
// or call an `automation` or an `action`.
//
// Its body is the statement language every logic and automation is written in
// (docs/public/language/memql.md#bodies).
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Call-graph contract (ADR §2 table) -- enforced by the call-graph validator
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Construct  | May call                                        | May NOT
//   -----------|-------------------------------------------------|---------
//   automation | logic, query, mutation, builtin, action,        | --
//              | sub-automation; publish; branch (if / switch),  |
//              | loop (for), fan-out (parallel)                  |
//
// Automations consume the layers above them and never the other way around:
// a logic/query/mutation/action never calls back into an automation.

// =============================================================================
//   1. Automation-level annotations
// =============================================================================
//
//   @trigger(...)           The reactive surface. EXACTLY ONE per automation
//                           (the only construct allowed to carry it).
//   @filter(row => ...)     A predicate over the triggering row: the
//                           automation runs only when it holds.
//   /// text                The description: a doc comment above the
//                           declaration (@description("text") is the same).
//   @disabled               Skip the automation at load. A construct is
//                           enabled by default; @enabled was the explicit-on
//                           no-op and is RETIRED (epic memql#5375).
//   @template               A work-spine template: invoked by the run that
//                           names it; it carries no @trigger.
//   @actor                  The body reads actor.*.
//   @mcp                    Promote the automation to the MCP surface.
//
// Trigger forms:
//   @trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:<ns>:<concept>")
//                           A graph event on a concept (node.created /
//                           node.updated / node.deleted).
//   @trigger(event="<topic>")
//                           A published topic, or system.startup /
//                           system.shutdown.
//   @trigger(schedule="<6-field cron>")
//                           Time-driven (sec min hour dom mon dow), e.g.
//                           @trigger(schedule="0 */10 * * * *") = every 10 min.
//
// =============================================================================
//   2. The args contract and the event
// =============================================================================
//
// The triggering row's payload is bound INTO the automation's `args { }`
// block and validated against it before the first statement runs
// (event-payload-binding ADR Decision 2):
//   - a missing required field / type mismatch / @enum miss / @maxLength
//     overrun / @pattern miss makes the automation REFUSE to fire -- a loud
//     Warn (automation / topic / field / reason) + a skip counter. Never a
//     silent nil, never a partial run.
//   - unknown extra payload fields are TOLERATED (tolerant-reader): not
//     bound, Debug-noted.
// The invoke-by-reference path validates its params against the SAME
// contract.
//
// Field grammar (identical to logic / query args):
//   <name> <type>[!] [@enum("a","b")] [@maxLength(N)] [@pattern("re")]
//                     [@minimum(N)] [@maximum(N)]
//   -- `!` (or @required) makes a field required; describe a field with a ///
//      doc comment above it. @default and @description are refused on an args
//      field: default in the body with `??`, `args.x ?? <v>`.
//
// Reading the fields inside the body: `args.<field>`, always. A bare field
// name is refused (body_unknown_name, whose hint says args.<field>), and so is
// a dotted `event.<field>` read (G5): `event` is the event itself, and an
// automation forwards it whole to a logic that wants it, `logic l(event: event)`.
// The logic declares `event object!` and reads args.event.payload.<field>.

/// On to-do creation, promote it into the Library Records lens. (The live
/// automation is dsl/library/automations.memql, indexTodoOnCreate.)
@trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:todos:todo")
automation referenceIndexTodo {
  args {
    id          any
    ownerUserId any
    title       any
  }

  persist := mutation createArtifact(
    sourceConceptRef: args.id,
    ownerUserId:      args.ownerUserId,
    lens:             "record",
    kind:             "todo",
    source:           "agent_generated",
    title:            args.title ?? "Untitled to-do",
    live:             false
  )
}

// =============================================================================
//   3. Statements
// =============================================================================
//
//   x := <kind> name(<named args>)        bind a call's value to x
//   x := <expression>                     bind a computed value
//   <kind> name(<named args>)             call; the value is not bound
//   if <cond> { } else if <cond> { } else { }
//                                         `else` on the closing brace's line
//   for x in <source> if <filter> { }     loop; x exists only inside
//   switch <subject> { case "a", "b" { } default { } }
//   parallel { branch a { } branch b { } } [wait any]
//   publish "<topic>" { <field>: <value>, ... }
//   return [<expression>]                 end the run early
//
// <kind> is query, mutation, logic, builtin, automation or action. Every
// argument is named (`name: value`), and a construct call is a statement of
// its own -- the whole right-hand side of `:=` or a line by itself, never
// nested in an expression (body_call_in_expression). One statement per line.
//
// Trailing clauses, written in this order:
//   on surface("<name>")   an action call: the surface it runs on
//   retry(<n>)             a call: retry a failure up to n more times
//   on error continue      a call, a for, a parallel: record the failure,
//                          leave the name absent and go on (stopping the run
//                          is the default and is never written)
//
// Names: a statement's name is its value -- a query's rows, a mutation's
// written row, a logic's return value, a builtin's result, an action's
// capability result. A name is read only after the statement that binds it
// (body_forward_reference). An if branch or a switch case runs at most once
// and shares the scope around it: a name bound in a branch that did not run
// reads absent, and the branches of one chain may each bind the same name. A
// loop body and a parallel branch have their own scope.
//
// CONDITION VOCABULARY (P4, #2371 -- enforced by the call-graph validator, the
// whole-tree contract test and the authoring sandbox at define time).
// A condition (an if, a for's `if` filter, a @filter) may use:
//   - a decided value: decide == <value>, gate.passed == true
//   - presence guards: <ref> != nil
//   - SINGLE-value fan-out equality: <field> == "<one literal>"
//   - boolean fields / == true / == false
// FINDINGS (policy in orchestration -- move it to a pure decide logic or a
// query pushdown):
//   - a same-field string-literal ||-vocabulary (x == "a" || x == "b")
//   - date math / defaults in conditions (addDuration, `??`, `+`)
// A switch SUBJECT is exempt: switching on a decided value IS the sanctioned
// fan-out shape.
//
// =============================================================================
//   4. A multi-statement orchestration (decide / persist / act / branch)
// =============================================================================
//
// The deployment bundle's automation, as dsl/deployment/automations.memql
// writes it. Engine mesh ONLY -- no bff / product nodes. Each action statement
// is one external capability on the cockpit/runner surface; the provider
// `switch` stays in ONE automation.

/// Deploy the MemQL ENGINE cluster: authorize -> record -> clone -> build ->
/// place (provider switch) -> gate -> finish -> roll back on a failed gate.
@trigger(event="deploy.requested")
automation referenceDeployEngineCluster {
  args {
    deploymentId         string!
    provider             enum("docker-local", "azure")!
    engineNodeTypes      []string!
    ref                  string!
    version              string!
    workdir              string!
    overlayPath          string
    digests              object
    previousDeploymentId string
    triggeredBy          string
    dryRun               boolean
  }

  authorize := logic deploymentForwardAllowed(event: event)
  record := mutation createDeployment(
    deploymentId: args.deploymentId,
    provider:     args.provider,
    version:      args.version,
    triggeredBy:  args.triggeredBy,
    status:       "in_progress",
    previousDeploymentId: args.previousDeploymentId
  )
  clone := action cloneRepoAtVersion(workdir: args.workdir, ref: args.ref, dryRun: args.dryRun)
  for nt in args.engineNodeTypes {
    action buildEngineImage(nodeType: nt, version: args.version, workdir: args.workdir, dryRun: args.dryRun)
  }
  for nt in args.engineNodeTypes if args.provider == "docker-local" {
    action importImageToK3d(nodeType: nt, cluster: "memql", version: args.version, dryRun: args.dryRun)
  }
  switch args.provider {
    case "docker-local" { }
    default {
      action pinOverlayDigests(overlayPath: args.overlayPath, digests: args.digests, dryRun: args.dryRun)
      action argoSync(app: "memql", revision: args.ref, dryRun: args.dryRun)
    }
  }
  gate := action runDeployGate(provider: args.provider, workdir: args.workdir, dryRun: args.dryRun)
  outcome := logic deployOutcomeLabel(passed: gate.passed)
  switch outcome {
    case "succeeded" {
      mutation updateDeploymentStatus(deploymentId: args.deploymentId, status: "succeeded")
      action notifyDeploy(status: "succeeded", deploymentId: args.deploymentId, dryRun: args.dryRun)
    }
    default {
      mutation updateDeploymentStatus(deploymentId: args.deploymentId, status: "failed")
      action notifyDeploy(status: "failed", deploymentId: args.deploymentId, dryRun: args.dryRun)
    }
  }
  rollbackTarget := logic deployRollbackTarget(passed: gate.passed, previousDeploymentId: args.previousDeploymentId)
  switch rollbackTarget {
    case "" { }
    default {
      action revertOverlay(overlayPath: args.overlayPath, toDeploymentId: rollbackTarget, dryRun: args.dryRun)
    }
  }
}

// A `for` source may be a collection chain from the in-memory collection /
// lambda library -- e.g. `for m in args.members.where(m => m.active) { ... }`.
// Lambda bodies stay PURE (no mutations / actions inside the lambda); the side
// effects live in the loop's statements. See `_logic.memql` §6.
//
// =============================================================================
//   5. Scheduled, with a publish
// =============================================================================

/// Every 10 min: mark departed cluster nodes as health='stopped', and say how
/// many went.
@trigger(schedule="0 */10 * * * *")
automation referencePruneStaleClusterNodes {
  stale := logic pruneStaleClusterNodes(event: event)
  for node in stale {
    mutation updateNodeHealth(id: node.id, health: "stopped", lastSeen: now) on error continue
  }
  publish "cluster.nodes.pruned" { count: stale.count() }
}

// =============================================================================
//   6. Preconditions (self-healing)
// =============================================================================
//
// A `precondition NAME { ... }` block sits after the args block and before the
// first statement: a deterministic boolean `check:` evaluated after the
// trigger fires and before any statement runs. It reads only the roots
// (args, actor, event, config, partition, now -- checked at load) and may
// call catalog functions such as var("NAME"); comparisons, && / || / !. A miss aborts the run as
// skipped and emits healing.precondition.missed. `literal:` names the
// machine-specific literal asserted, `description:` the context.

/// Route a submitted request by the submitter's role -- only one that carries
/// its id.
@trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:forge:request")
automation referenceRouteRequest {
  args {
    id            any
    submitterRole any
  }
  precondition hasId {
    check: args.id != nil
    literal: id
    description: "Route only a request that carries its id."
  }
  decide := logic requestRouteStatus(submitterRole: args.submitterRole)
  mutation advanceRequest(requestId: args.id, status: decide)
}

// =============================================================================
//   7. NON-COMPLIANT examples
// =============================================================================
//
// 7a. A construct call nested in an expression (body_call_in_expression). A
//     side effect that is not a statement is not journaled, previewed or
//     retried: bind it, then read the name.
//
//   total := 1 + query openTickets().count()      // <- refused
//
// 7b. A trigger on a non-automation. `@trigger` is automation-only -- a
//     logic/query/mutation carrying one is refused (see `_logic.memql` §5c).
//
// 7c. The retired body forms, each refused at parse by name and rewritten by
//     `memqlmigrate --rewrite=bodies`:
//       - `step NAME { <call> }` blocks (body_step_retired)
//                                          -> `NAME := <call>`
//       - a `body { }` block (body_block_retired)
//                                          -> the statements, after args { }
//       - the terse `automation X @trigger(...) => logic Y` header
//         (body_terse_retired)            -> an automation whose one statement
//                                             is `logic Y(event: event)`
//       - `steps.x.result` / `x.result` (body_steps_reference_retired)
//                                          -> `x`
//       - `forEach x in s where f { }` (body_foreach_retired)
//                                          -> `for x in s if f { }`
//       - `publishEvent(...)` (body_publish_event_retired)
//                                          -> `publish "<topic>" { ... }`
//       - a call with no kind, the argument pun `logic l(event)`
//         (body_call_kind_missing, body_positional_argument)
//                                          -> `logic l(event: event)`
//       - `partition="*"` on @trigger (trigger_partition_retired) -> delete it
//       - `@schedule(cron="...")` (trigger_schedule_synonym_retired)
//                                          -> `@trigger(schedule="...")`
//     The step bodies' accessors are refused at parse the same way
//     (body_accessor_retired), with no rewrite: `step("x")` -> `x`,
//     `input()` -> `args.<name>`, `item()` / `index()` -> the loop's own
//     name, `for x in s`.
//
// See `_logic.memql` for pure decisions and `_action.memql` for the
// external-capability construct each `action` statement calls.
// =============================================================================
